.nh
.TH ttyd 1 "September 2016" ttyd "User Manual"

.SH NAME
.PP
ttyd - Share your terminal over the web


.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
\fBttyd\fP [options] <command> [<arguments...>]


.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
ttyd is a command-line tool for sharing terminal over the web that runs in *nix and windows systems, with the following features:

.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Rust backend built on Tokio and Axum
.IP \(bu 2
Fully-featured terminal based on Xterm.js with CJK (Chinese, Japanese, Korean) and IME support
.IP \(bu 2
Graphical ZMODEM integration with lrzsz support
.IP \(bu 2
Sixel image output support
.IP \(bu 2
TLS and mutual TLS support based on rustls
.IP \(bu 2
Run any custom command with options
.IP \(bu 2
Basic authentication and reverse-proxy header authentication
.IP \(bu 2
Resumable sessions: reloading a tab reattaches to the same PTY with recent output replayed
.IP \(bu 2
Cross platform: Linux, macOS, and Windows (ConPTY). On Windows, -s does not deliver a POSIX signal and there is no process group; the child is terminated instead

.RE


.SH OPTIONS
.PP
-p, --port 
      Port to listen (default: 7681, use \fB\fC0\fR for random port)

.PP
-i, --interface 
      Network interface to bind (eg: eth0), or UNIX domain socket path (eg: /var/run/ttyd.sock)

.PP
-U, --socket-owner
      User owner of the UNIX domain socket file, when enabled (eg: user:group)

.PP
-c, --credential USER[:PASSWORD]
      Credential for Basic Authentication (format: username:password)

.PP
-H, --auth-header 
      HTTP Header name for auth proxy, this will configure ttyd to let a HTTP reverse proxy handle authentication

.PP
-u, --uid 
      User id to run with

.PP
-g, --gid 
      Group id to run with

.PP
-s, --signal 
      Signal to send to the command when exit it (default: 1, SIGHUP). On Windows the signal is not delivered and there is no process group; the child is terminated instead

.PP
-w, --cwd 
      Working directory to be set for the child program

.PP
-a, --url-arg
      Allow client to send command line arguments in URL (eg: http://localhost:7681?arg=foo&arg=bar)

.PP
-W, --writable
      Allow clients to write to the TTY (readonly by default)

.PP
-t, --client-option 
      Send option to client (format: key=value), repeat to add more options, see \fBCLIENT OPTIONS\fP for details

.PP
-T, --terminal-type
      Terminal type to report, default: xterm-256color

.PP
-O, --check-origin
      Do not allow websocket connection from different origin

.PP
-m, --max-clients
      Maximum clients to support (default: 0, no limit)

.PP
-o, --once
      Accept only one client and exit on disconnection

.PP
-q, --exit-no-conn
      Exit on all clients disconnection

.PP
-B, --browser
      Open terminal with the default system browser

.PP
-I, --index 
      Custom index.html path

.PP
-b, --base-path
      Expected base path for requests coming from a reverse proxy (eg: /mounted/here, max length: 128)

.PP
-P, --ping-interval
      Websocket ping interval(sec) (default: 5)

.PP
-f, --srv-buf-size
      Maximum bytes read from the PTY at once, a larger value may improve throughput (default: 4096)

.PP
--session-timeout
      Seconds to keep a detached session alive (default: 30, 0 disables persistence)

.PP
--session-buffer-size
      Bytes of output retained for session replay (default: 4194304)

.PP
--history-limit
      Ended sessions to keep for read-only replay (default: 20, 0 disables history)

.PP
--http-transfer
      Enable the HTTP file transfer endpoints. Requires -W.

.PP
--http-transfer-dir <dir>
      Upload destination when the shell cwd cannot be followed (Windows, macOS, probe failure, or --no-http-follow-cwd). Defaults to -w / the process cwd.

.PP
--http-transfer-root <dir>
      Restrict every file operation to this canonicalized directory tree. By default any shell cwd may be followed.

.PP
--http-transfer-max-upload <bytes>
      Maximum size of one uploaded file (default: 67108864).

.PP
--http-transfer-session-quota <bytes>
      Approximate per-directory quota; 0 disables the check (default: 268435456).

.PP
--no-http-follow-cwd
      Always use --http-transfer-dir instead of following the shell cwd.

.PP
-6, --ipv6
      Enable IPv6 support

.PP
-S, --ssl
      Enable SSL

.PP
-C, --ssl-cert 
      SSL certificate file path

.PP
-K, --ssl-key 
      SSL key file path

.PP
-A, --ssl-ca 
      SSL CA file path for client certificate verification

.PP
-d, --debug 
      Set log level (default: 7)

.PP
-v, --version
      Print the version and exit

.PP
-h, --help
      Print this text and exit


.SH CLIENT OPTIONS
.PP
ttyd has a mechanism to pass server side command-line arguments to the browser page which is called \fBclient options\fP:

.PP
.RS

.nf
-t, --client-option     Send option to client (format: key=value), repeat to add more options

.fi
.RE

.SH Basic usage
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC-t rendererType=webgl\fR: use the \fB\fCwebgl\fR renderer for xterm.js (default: \fB\fCwebgl\fR, falls back to \fB\fCdom\fR; \fB\fCcanvas\fR is deprecated and also uses \fB\fCdom\fR)
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC-t disableLeaveAlert=true\fR: disable the leave page alert
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC-t disableResizeOverlay=true\fR: disable the terminal resize overlay
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC-t disableReconnect=true\fR: prevent the terminal from reconnecting on connection error/close
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC-t enableZmodem=true\fR: enable ZMODEM
\[la]https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ZMODEM\[ra] / lrzsz
\[la]https://ohse.de/uwe/software/lrzsz.html\[ra] file transfer support
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC-t enableTrzsz=true\fR: enable trzsz
\[la]https://trzsz.github.io\[ra] file transfer support
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC-t enableSixel=true\fR: enable Sixel
\[la]https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sixel\[ra] image output support (Usage
\[la]https://saitoha.github.io/libsixel/\[ra])
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC-t closeOnDisconnect=true\fR: close the terminal on disconnection, this will disable reconnect
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC-t titleFixed=hello\fR: set a fixed title for the browser window
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC-t fontSize=20\fR: change the font size of the terminal
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC-t unicodeVersion=11\fR: set xterm unicode support level (default: 11, use 6 to disable unicode addon)
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC-t trzszDragInitTimeout=3000\fR: set the timeout in milliseconds for initializing drag and drop files to upload. (default: 3000)
.IP \(bu 2
.IP \(bu 2

.RE

.SH Advanced usage
.PP
You can use the client option to change all the settings of xterm defined in ITerminalOptions
\[la]https://xtermjs.org/docs/api/terminal/interfaces/iterminaloptions/\[ra], examples:

.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC-t cursorStyle=bar\fR: set cursor style to \fB\fCbar\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC-t lineHeight=1.5\fR: set line-height to \fB\fC1.5\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fC-t 'theme={"background": "green"}'\fR: set background color to \fB\fCgreen\fR

.RE

.PP
to try the example options above, run:

.PP
.RS

.nf
ttyd -t cursorStyle=bar -t lineHeight=1.5 -t 'theme={"background": "green"}' bash

.fi
.RE


.SH SESSION PERSISTENCE
.PP
By default a detached PTY is kept for 30 seconds. Reloading the page reattaches to the same shell, editor, or running command, and up to 4 MiB of recent output is replayed into the terminal. The session id is stored in sessionStorage, so each tab has its own session and opening a new tab starts a fresh command.

.PP
\fB\fC--session-timeout <seconds>\fR changes how long a detached session is kept. \fB\fC0\fR disables persistence and terminates the command immediately after the last client disconnects. \fB\fC--session-buffer-size <bytes>\fR changes the in-memory replay buffer size.

.PP
Add \fB\fC?sessionTimeout=N\fR to the page URL to override the timeout for that tab (maximum 3600 seconds). The override cannot re-enable persistence when the server was started with \fB\fC--session-timeout 0\fR. \fB\fC-q\fR / \fB\fC--exit-no-conn\fR and \fB\fC-o\fR / \fB\fC--once\fR keep their existing semantics and may exit before a refreshed page can reconnect.


.SH SESSION HISTORY
.PP
Ended sessions are kept in memory for read-only replay. When a session exits, expires, or is deleted, ttyd copies its id, owner, command, exit code, end time, and current replay buffer into a history table before reclaiming the PTY. The PTY is not retained, so a history entry can only be replayed read-only and cannot be reattached as the original shell. The oldest record is dropped when the limit is exceeded, and history disappears when the process restarts. Stored output is bounded by \fB\fC--session-buffer-size\fR.

.PP
\fB\fC--history-limit <count>\fR sets how many ended sessions are kept (default: 20). \fB\fC0\fR disables history.

.PP
A history replay opens \fB\fC/ws\fR with the normal handshake JSON plus \fB\fC"attachOnly": true\fR. If the id exists, the server replays the stored output frames and then sends frame \fB\fC4\fR with \fB\fC{"readonly":true,"exitCode":N}\fR; afterwards it discards input and creates no PTY. If the id is not in history, the connection closes without starting a shell. A normal reconnect does not send \fB\fCattachOnly\fR, and an expired or archived id keeps today's behavior of starting a new session.

.PP
The session API is served under the base path set by \fB\fC-b\fR:

.RS
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fCGET /sessions\fR returns only the current user's running sessions and history. It requires authentication (\fB\fC-c\fR, \fB\fC--credential-file\fR, \fB\fCTTYD_CREDENTIAL\fR, \fB\fC--random-credential\fR, or \fB\fC-H\fR); when no authentication is configured it returns \fB\fC404\fR so session ids are not exposed. With \fB\fC-c\fR the credential is shared, so every holder sees the same list.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fCGET /sessions/{id}\fR returns metadata only, without the replay body. Without authentication, knowing the id is enough, like connecting to \fB\fC/ws\fR by id. With authentication the session must belong to the current user, otherwise \fB\fC403\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fCDELETE /sessions/{id}\fR kills and archives a running session, or deletes a history record. With authentication it must belong to the current user, otherwise \fB\fC403\fR. It returns \fB\fC204\fR on success and \fB\fC404\fR when the id is missing.

.RE

.PP
Timestamps are Unix epoch milliseconds (\fB\fCendedAt\fR). Killed or expired sessions record exit code \fB\fC-1\fR. The browser session panel shows Running and History entries and only appears when \fB\fCGET /sessions\fR is available, that is when authentication is configured.


.SH FILE TRANSFER AND SIXEL
.PP
ZMODEM, trzsz, and Sixel are off until a \fB\fC-t\fR client option turns them on. File transfer and the probe reply used by \fB\fCimg2sixel\fR / \fB\fClsix\fR both have to be written back to the PTY, so these examples include \fB\fC-W\fR. Install each program in the environment of the command ttyd starts, then try the commands by hand. The HTTP file transfer path below is built into ttyd and needs nothing installed remotely.

.SS HTTP file transfer
.PP
Enable it with \fB\fC--http-transfer -W\fR; it is off by default and works over plain HTTP:

.PP
.RS

.nf
ttyd --http-transfer -W bash
ttyd --http-transfer -W --http-transfer-dir /srv/uploads bash

.fi
.RE

.PP
\fBUpload\fR: click the tab bar "Upload" button or drop files onto the terminal. The dialog shows the destination before the native file picker opens. On Linux the destination follows the shell cwd through \fB\fC/proc/<pid>/cwd\fR; when that is unavailable (Windows, macOS, ConPTY, probe failure, or \fB\fC--no-http-follow-cwd\fR) it uses \fB\fC--http-transfer-dir <dir>\fR, then \fB\fC-w\fR / the process cwd.

.PP
\fBDownload\fR: click the tab bar "Download" button, type a server file path (absolute, or relative to the destination) and press Download. Terminal input is never intercepted \(em \fB\fCttyd-*\fR is not a command.

.PP
Two routes are served under the base path set by \fB\fC-b\fR, both requiring \fB\fCX-TTYD-Transfer: 1\fR and bound to a live session id and its owner: \fB\fCPOST /sessions/{id}/files?name=<file>\fR uploads a raw \fB\fCapplication/octet-stream\fR body (\fB\fC409\fR on an existing name, \fB\fC413\fR over the size limit), and \fB\fCGET /sessions/{id}/download?path=<path>\fR downloads any file. Symlinks, directories and paths outside \fB\fC--http-transfer-root\fR are rejected (\fB\fC403\fR). Without \fB\fC--http-transfer\fR the routes return \fB\fC404\fR; without \fB\fC-W\fR they return \fB\fC403\fR. Existing names are never overwritten; there is no resume or chunked concurrency. Without a root, any file readable by the ttyd process can be downloaded \(em use authentication on untrusted networks.

.PP
ZMODEM requires lrzsz (\fB\fCsz\fR and \fB\fCrz\fR):

.PP
.RS

.nf
ttyd -W -t enableZmodem=true bash

.fi
.RE

.PP
Run \fB\fCsz file\fR to download a file in the browser. Run \fB\fCrz\fR and choose files to upload. Ctrl+C, or Cancel in the dialog, restores terminal input.

.PP
trzsz (\fB\fCtrz\fR and \fB\fCtsz\fR):

.PP
.RS

.nf
ttyd -W -t enableTrzsz=true bash

.fi
.RE

.PP
\fB\fCtrz\fR uploads from the browser and \fB\fCtsz file\fR downloads a file. Dropping files onto the terminal also uploads them; ttyd starts \fB\fCtrz\fR and waits \fB\fC-t trzszDragInitTimeout\fR milliseconds (default 3000) for the server. ZMODEM and trzsz can both be enabled. ZMODEM still works when no trzsz transfer is in progress.

.PP
The File System Access API used by trzsz only exists on HTTPS or \fB\fClocalhost\fR. On a plain HTTP address, such as a LAN IP, ttyd substitutes a native file picker: uploads still work, directory picks use \fB\fCwebkitdirectory\fR, and \fB\fCtsz\fR downloads every file separately instead of writing into a chosen folder. Use \fB\fC-S -C <cert> -K <key>\fR or an HTTPS reverse proxy for the full behaviour.

.PP
Input frames are capped at 256 KiB by the server, so the browser limits a trzsz data chunk to 96 KiB before encoding. Raising \fB\fC-f\fR / \fB\fC--srv-buf-size\fR does not change that limit; it only controls how much the PTY delivers per read.

.PP
Sixel, for img2sixel and lsix:

.PP
.RS

.nf
ttyd -W -t enableSixel=true bash

.fi
.RE

.PP
\fB\fCimg2sixel image.png\fR draws an image. Use a larger \fB\fC-f\fR (for example \fB\fC-f 65536\fR) for a large image. With Sixel enabled, the primary DA reply (\fB\fCCSI c\fR) includes parameter \fB\fC4\fR. The secondary DA reply (\fB\fCCSI > c\fR) is unchanged.

.PP
ConPTY on Windows does not pass these binary protocols through unchanged; use them on Linux or macOS. On Windows, ttyd sends \fB\fCisWindows=true\fR. If the command is a POSIX shell, add \fB\fC-t isWindows=false\fR.


.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
ttyd starts web server at port 7681 by default, you can use the -p option to change it, the command will be started with arguments as options. For example, run:

.PP
.RS

.nf
ttyd -p 8080 bash -x

.fi
.RE

.PP
Then open http://localhost:8080 with a browser, you will get a bash shell with debug mode enabled. More examples:

.RS
.IP \(bu 2
If you want to login with your system accounts on the web browser, run \fB\fCttyd login\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
You can even run a non-shell command like vim, try: \fB\fCttyd vim\fR, the web browser will show you a vim editor.
.IP \(bu 2
Sharing single process with multiple clients: \fB\fCttyd tmux new -A -s ttyd vim\fR, run \fB\fCtmux new -A -s ttyd\fR to connect to the tmux session from terminal.

.RE


.SH SSL how-to
.PP
Generate SSL CA and self signed server/client certificates:

.PP
.RS

.nf
# CA certificate (FQDN must be different from server/client)
openssl genrsa -out ca.key 2048
openssl req -new -x509 -days 365 -key ca.key -subj "/C=CN/ST=GD/L=SZ/O=Acme, Inc./CN=Acme Root CA" -out ca.crt

# server certificate (for multiple domains, change subjectAltName to: DNS:example.com,DNS:www.example.com)
openssl req -newkey rsa:2048 -nodes -keyout server.key -subj "/C=CN/ST=GD/L=SZ/O=Acme, Inc./CN=localhost" -out server.csr
openssl x509 -sha256 -req -extfile <(printf "subjectAltName=DNS:localhost") -days 365 -in server.csr -CA ca.crt -CAkey ca.key -CAcreateserial -out server.crt

# client certificate (the p12/pem format may be useful for some clients)
openssl req -newkey rsa:2048 -nodes -keyout client.key -subj "/C=CN/ST=GD/L=SZ/O=Acme, Inc./CN=client" -out client.csr
openssl x509 -req -days 365 -in client.csr -CA ca.crt -CAkey ca.key -CAcreateserial -out client.crt
openssl pkcs12 -export -clcerts -in client.crt -inkey client.key -out client.p12
openssl pkcs12 -in client.p12 -out client.pem -clcerts

.fi
.RE

.PP
Then start ttyd:

.PP
.RS

.nf
ttyd --ssl --ssl-cert server.crt --ssl-key server.key --ssl-ca ca.crt bash

.fi
.RE

.PP
You may want to test the client certificate verification with \fIcurl\fP(1):

.PP
.RS

.nf
curl --insecure --cert client.p12[:password] -v https://localhost:7681

.fi
.RE

.PP
If you don't want to enable client certificate verification, remove the \fB\fC--ssl-ca\fR option.


.SH Docker and ttyd
.PP
Docker containers are jailed environments which are more secure, this is useful for protecting the host system, you may use ttyd with docker like this:

.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Sharing single docker container with multiple clients: docker run -it --rm -p 7681:7681 tsl0922/ttyd.
.IP \(bu 2
Creating new docker container for each client: ttyd docker run -it --rm ubuntu.

.RE


.SH Nginx reverse proxy
.PP
Sample config to proxy ttyd under the \fB\fC/ttyd\fR path:

.PP
.RS

.nf
location ~ ^/ttyd(.*)$ {
    proxy_http_version 1.1;
    proxy_set_header Host $host;
    proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
    proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
    proxy_set_header Upgrade $http_upgrade;
    proxy_set_header Connection "upgrade";
    proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:7681/$1;
}

.fi
.RE


